Correct Qt Multimedia's Wasm startup media lifecycle for Qt 6.11.1.

HTMLMediaElement.load() resets the media element and queues an "emptied"
event before the new resource starts loading. That event means readyState
became HAVE_NOTHING; it is not the natural end of playback. Keep "ended" as
the sole browser event that maps to QMediaPlayer::EndOfMedia and use
"emptied" only to revoke readiness. The subsequent "loadstart" event owns
the transition to LoadingMedia for a nonempty replacement source.

Also backport the QWasmMediaPlayer::stop() part of upstream Qt commit
4e61fa7da7e7db730e6e4762839de24f99e7803c. QMediaPlayer::setSource()
idempotently stops an idle backend before installing the new source, so that
path must not emit a warning.

Backport upstream Qt commit
8a2093d1dda70eae63a0522537c93605f8932041 as well. Natural "ended"
notification must update status without recursively tearing down the element;
explicit source cleanup owns teardown. The stop implementation also has to
guard re-entry before touching members and must not dereference the optional
camera/capture media stream for ordinary URL video playback.

The null guard relies on the pointer initialization from upstream Qt commit
d6f64920e9024de8cf0d8761f304fc8999700783. Without that initializer,
ordinary URL playback would read an indeterminate pointer while stopping.

Finally, destruction must be the terminal resource boundary. A naturally
completed QMediaPlayer is already in PlaybackCompleted, so its stop() path
intentionally does nothing and the default QWasmVideoOutput destructor leaves
the HTMLMediaElement connected with its resource loaded. Tear down stream and
DOM ownership in QWasmVideoOutput destruction, after latching terminal state,
so normal player lifetime alone releases the browser resource without
stopping a capture stream that another output may share.

